WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.663
Book Three, Proposition Fifteen.

2
00:00:02.663 --> 00:00:12.198
Of straight lines in a circle the diameter is greatest, and of the rest the nearer to the center is always greater than the more remote.

3
00:00:12.198 --> 00:00:21.732
Let A B C D be a circle, let A D be its diameter and E the center, and let the chord B C be nearer to the center E, and F G more remote.

4
00:00:21.732 --> 00:00:27.130
We claim that the diameter A D is greatest, and that B C is greater than F G.

5
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:33.461
From the center E, draw E H and E K perpendicular to B C and F G.

6
00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:44.647
Since B C is nearer to the center and F G more remote, by Definition Three Five, the perpendicular E K is greater than E H.

7
00:00:44.647 --> 00:00:48.830
On E K, cut off E L equal to E H by One Three.

8
00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:57.560
Through L, draw the chord M N at right angles to E K, and join the radii M E, E N, F E, and E G.

9
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:06.712
Because the perpendiculars E H and E L are equal, by Three Fourteen the chords B C and M N are also equal.

10
00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:14.538
Now the radii A E and E D equal M E and E N, so the diameter A D equals the sum of M E and E N.

11
00:01:14.538 --> 00:01:27.141
By One Twenty, in triangle M E N, the two sides M E and E N together are greater than M N—and since M N equals B C, the diameter A D is greater than B C.

12
00:01:27.141 --> 00:01:41.310
Furthermore, in triangles M E N and F E G, the two radii M E and E N equal the two radii F E and E G, while the central angle M E N is greater than the central angle F E G.

13
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:52.184
By One Twenty-Four, the hinge theorem, the base M N is greater than the base F G; and since B C equals M N, B C is greater than F G.

14
00:01:52.184 --> 00:02:01.081
Therefore, the diameter A D is greatest, and the nearer chord B C is greater than the more remote chord F G.

15
00:02:01.081 --> 00:02:03.140
As was required to prove.

16
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:22.959
Heath highlights the elegance of Euclid's construction in Proposition Three Fifteen: rather than comparing the squares on the half-chords directly via One Forty-Seven—as Theodosius and Robert Simson later did—Euclid transfers the nearer chord B C by rotation to the auxiliary chord M N perpendicular to E K.

17
00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:32.310
Once B C is aligned with F G along the same central ray E K, the comparison reduces immediately to Book One's triangle inequality and hinge theorem.

18
00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:44.000
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixteen unites eleven direct prerequisites across sixty-five ancestor nodes, completing the foundational chord-metric theorems of Book Three.

19
